    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The New Testament is a collection of 27 Christian texts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a>, made up of four gospels, Acts, epistles and Revelation, and forms the second division of the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>.</li><li>Its canon developed gradually over the first few centuries through debate, and Athanasius listed the 27 books in 367; scholars still disagree on the dates and authors of many of them.</li><li>It survives in thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and translations, and textual criticism uses them to reconstruct the earliest recoverable text.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Irenaeus, writing around 180 CE, argued that there could be neither more nor fewer than four gospels, because there are four zones of the world and four principal winds.</p></aside>
    </section>

