    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 52</span><span class="rf-text">1 Thessalonians, likely the earliest of Paul's letters, is written</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 140</span><span class="rf-text">Marcion draws up a canon of a modified version of Luke and ten of Paul's letters, and rejects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 180</span><span class="rf-text">Irenaeus asserts that the four gospels are the only legitimate ones in Against Heresies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 300</span><span class="rf-text">Eusebius lists the accepted, disputed and rejected writings in his Ecclesiastical History</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">367</span><span class="rf-text">Athanasius gives the list of the 27 books in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> letter and calls them canonised</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">397</span><span class="rf-text">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> accepts a summary of the lost acts of the Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a> of 393</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1522</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s translation of the New Testament appears</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1611</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">Authorized Version</a>, also called the King James Version, is published</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/septuagint"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/septuagint.jpg" alt="Septuagint" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Vaticanus_(1_Esdras_1-55_to_2-5)_(The_S.S._Teacher%27s_Edition-The_Holy_Bible).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The New Testament is the second division of the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. Together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a>, which is based primarily on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, it makes up the sacred scripture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Its name rests on the idea of a new covenant that Christians believe completes or fulfils the covenant God made with Israel on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. Christians have traditionally found this new covenant foretold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-jeremiah">Book of Jeremiah</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> traditionally disagrees.</p><p>The word "testament" has a history of its own. The Hebrew word brit means 'alliance, covenant, pact'. The Jewish translators of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>, working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE, rendered it with the Greek diathēkē, which in the Greek world usually meant a will left after a person's death. Scholars debate why they chose it. Christian theology has understood the choice to give the covenant something of the character of a will left by the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p><p>The phrase as a name for a collection of Christian writings can be traced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tertullian">Tertullian</a>, who in Against Marcion, written c. 208 CE, spoke of the Divine Word as doubly edged "with the two testaments of the law and the gospel". By the 4th century an Old and a New Testament were established, though not yet their exact contents.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle.jpg" alt="Paul the Apostle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresco_of_Saint_Paul_at_Ephesus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The New Testament is a collection of 27 Christian texts written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> by various authors. Each of the four gospels narrates the life, death and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resurrection-of-jesus">resurrection of Jesus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>, although Mark in its original text ends with the empty tomb and has no account of appearances after the resurrection. "Gospel" comes from the Old English gōd-spell, "good news", which translates the Greek euangélion. Matthew, Mark and Luke are called the Synoptic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> because they share many stories, often in the same order and sometimes in the same words. John is quite different and begins with a philosophical prologue.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> is a narrative of the apostles' ministry after the death and resurrection of Jesus, and functions as a sequel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>. Thirteen letters present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a> as their author: nine to churches and four to individuals. Hebrews is anonymous. The Catholic epistles, a name meaning "general" or "universal", are James, 1 and 2 Peter, 1, 2 and 3 John, and Jude. The Book of Revelation, also called the Apocalypse of John, opens with letters to seven congregations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and then takes the form of an apocalypse, a "revealing" of divine prophecy.</p><p>The theologian Frank Stagg listed concerns common to its writers, among them that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a> is central and that his death and resurrection are at the heart of the whole event.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;New Testament&#8221; cites where it covers Establishing a critical text:</p><ul><li>Bruce M. Metzger, The Text of the New Testament: Its Transmission, Corruption, and Restoration (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koine-greek.jpg" alt="Koine Greek" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek_Script_from_Rosetta_Stone_(36140196440).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Literary analysis suggests that many of the texts were written in the mid to late first century. The earliest are the letters of Paul, and 1 Thessalonians is likely the earliest of them, written around 52 CE. There is no scholarly consensus on the date of the latest text. John A. T. Robinson, William F. Albright and others dated many or all of the books before 70 CE, while Bart D. Ehrman and Stephen L. Harris date some much later. Richard Pervo dated Luke–Acts to c. 115 CE.</p><p>The gospels are anonymous, as biographies of the time often were, and their names come from church tradition. Scholars hold that the names were fixed to them by the mid 2nd century. Most scholars hold to Marcan priority, the view that Mark was written first. Many argue that Matthew and Luke used Mark and a hypothetical Q source, though alternatives without Q are gaining ground. Most scholars understand the gospels as Greco-Roman biography. Seven letters of Paul are generally classed as undisputed: Romans, 1 and 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians, 1 Thessalonians and Philemon. The authorship of six others is disputed, and most scholars regard the Pastoral epistles as pseudepigrapha.</p><p>It is generally agreed that the historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> primarily spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, while the books were written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a>. Scholars differ on why its style departs from that of literary Greek of the period.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;New Testament&#8221; cites where it covers Authors, Gospels, Acts, Pauline epistles:</p><ul><li>Bart D. Ehrman, The Lost Gospel of Judas Iscariot: A New Look at Betrayer and Betrayed (Oxford University Press, 2006)</li><li>Hugo Mendez, The Gospel of John: A New History (Oxford University Press, 2025)</li><li>Chris Keith, The Gospel as Manuscript: An Early History of the Jesus Tradition as Material Artifact (Oxford University Press, 2020)</li><li>Mark A. Powell, Introducing the New Testament: A Historical, Literary, and Theological Survey (Baker Academic, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/origen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/origen.jpg" alt="Origen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Origen3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The canon formed through a complex and lengthy process. Writings attributed to the apostles circulated among the earliest communities, and Paul's letters were circulating, perhaps in collected forms, by the end of the 1st century. Around 140 CE Marcion accepted only a version of Luke and ten of Paul's letters and rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> entirely; other Christians largely rejected his canon. Some scholars have argued that the church formulated its canon partly in response to him. In the mid 2nd century Justin Martyr mentioned "memoirs of the apostles" read on Sunday beside the "writings of the prophets". Irenaeus asserted a canon of four gospels around 180.</p><p>By the early 200s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> may have been using the same 27 books, though Hebrews, James, 2 Peter, 2 and 3 John and Revelation were still disputed. Eusebius, c. 300, sorted writings into accepted, disputed and rejected. Athanasius's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> letter of 367 listed the 27 books. The Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a> (393), whose acts are lost, may have been the first council to accept the canon, and the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> accepted a summary of its acts in 397 and 419. Lee Martin McDonald has written that the councils recognised books already prominent through use.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john.jpg" alt="Gospel of John" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JRL19060215_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RylandsImaging, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like other ancient literature, the text was preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. The earliest date from the late 2nd to early 3rd century, with the possible exception of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> 52, whose early date has recently been called into question. Papyrus 90, 98 and 104 have also been thought to date as early as the 2nd century. The New Testament survives in more than 5,800 Greek manuscripts, 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> manuscripts and 9,300 in other ancient languages, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Slavic, Ethiopic and Armenian. Church writings also quote it so widely that the whole text could be assembled from them.</p><p>The manuscripts contain variants, almost all of them trivial matters of spelling, punctuation or grammar. The two most commonly cited of some importance are the last verses of the Gospel of Mark and the story of the woman taken in adultery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a>. Many scholars regard the Johannine Comma in 1 John as a later addition. Bart D. Ehrman notes that scribal additions are often found in late medieval manuscripts but not in earlier ones.</p><p>Translations began at the end of the 2nd century, first into Syriac, Latin and Coptic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> urged a return to the original Greek, which began modern textual criticism.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;New Testament&#8221; cites where it covers Early manuscripts, Textual variation, Interpolations:</p><ul><li>Bruce M. Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the New Testament (German Bible Society, 1994)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-james-version.jpg" alt="King James Version" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">King James Version</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KJV-King-James-Version-Bible-first-edition-title-page-1611.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Church of England, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The 16th century brought an explosion of vernacular translations: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s (1522), William Tyndale's (1526) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">Authorized Version</a>, or King James Version (1611). Later translations, among them the Revised Standard Version (1946), the New International Version (1973) and the English Standard Version (2001), have largely used critical editions of the Greek text as their base.</p><p>Christian churches hold the text in different ways. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> teaches that divine revelation has one common source and two modes of transmission, Scripture and Tradition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Kallistos Ware says that for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> there is only one source, Holy Tradition, within which Scripture exists. Protestants, following sola scriptura, derive doctrine from scripture alone. Evangelicals hold it to be inerrant, as in the Chicago Statement on Biblical Inerrancy (1978), while Presbyterians generally do not insist on inerrancy. Latter-day Saints accept it "as far as it is translated correctly".</p><p>Most of its influence on the arts has come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and Revelation. Earliest Christian art depicted the raising of Lazarus, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptism-of-jesus">baptism of Jesus</a> and the Good Shepherd. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heliand">Heliand</a> and Passion plays followed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the Passion has been a theme in film since La Passion (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, 1903).</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The New Testament is the second division of the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, a collection of 27 Greek texts that includes four gospels, Acts, letters and Revelation. Its canon formed over several centuries, and it survives in thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> studied by textual critics.</em></p>

